# Component Principles

Every component should:

* Have one responsibility.
* Be reusable.
* Be composable.
* Be predictable.
* Be easy to test.
* Be easy to understand.

Prefer:

* Composition over inheritance
* Small focused components
* Explicit APIs
* Declarative code

Avoid:

* Large monolithic components
* Business logic inside UI components
* Duplicated code
* Excessive prop drilling

---

# React Best Practices

Use:

* Functional components
* React Hooks
* TypeScript
* Named exports where appropriate

Avoid:

* Class components
* Unnecessary useEffect
* Derived state
* any type
* Inline business logic inside JSX

Keep:

* Props minimal
* State close to where it is used
* Side effects isolated
* Components pure whenever possible

---

# File Organization

Recommended structure:

```
src/
  components/
    atoms/
    molecules/
    organisms/
    templates/

  features/

  pages/

  hooks/

  services/

  utils/

  types/

  constants/

  assets/
```

Co-locate:

* Tests
* Styles
* Stories
* Documentation

with their component whenever practical.

---

# Styling

Follow the project's styling system consistently.

Prefer:

* CSS Modules
* Tailwind
* Styled Components
* Design System tokens

Avoid:

* Large inline style objects
* Hard-coded spacing
* Hard-coded colors
* Magic numbers

Use:

* Responsive layouts
* Flexbox
* CSS Grid
* Relative units (rem/em)

---

# Accessibility (WCAG 2.2 AA)

Every generated UI must satisfy:

* WCAG 2.2 AA
* ADA
* Section 508

Accessibility is mandatory.

---

## Semantic HTML

Always prefer semantic elements.

Use:

* button
* nav
* main
* section
* article
* form
* label
* header
* footer
* aside

Avoid replacing semantic elements with div or span.

---

## Keyboard Accessibility

Every interactive element must support:

* Tab
* Shift + Tab
* Enter
* Space
* Escape (dialogs)
* Arrow keys where appropriate

Never create keyboard traps.

---

## Focus Management

Always:

* Preserve visible focus outlines.
* Restore focus after closing dialogs.
* Maintain logical tab order.
* Support keyboard-only users.

---

## Forms

Every form should:

* Have associated labels.
* Display inline validation.
* Associate errors using aria-describedby.
* Clearly indicate required fields.
* Never rely only on placeholders.

---

## Buttons & Links

Buttons:

* Trigger actions.

Links:

* Navigate.

Never use clickable div or span elements.

Interactive targets should be at least **44 × 44 CSS pixels** where practical.

---

## Images

Decorative images:

```
alt=""
```

Informative images:

Provide meaningful alt text.

Complex graphics:

Provide accessible descriptions.

---

## Color & Contrast

Meet WCAG AA:

* 4.5:1 text contrast
* 3:1 large text and UI controls

Never rely on color alone to communicate meaning.

---

## Motion

Respect:

```
prefers-reduced-motion
```

Avoid unnecessary animations.

Animations should never block task completion.

---

## Responsive Design

Use:

* Mobile-first design
* Flexible layouts
* CSS Grid
* Flexbox

Support:

* Mobile
* Tablet
* Desktop

No horizontal scrolling.

Support browser zoom up to 200%.

---

## Dialogs

Dialogs should:

* Trap focus while open.
* Close with Escape.
* Restore focus to the trigger.
* Include appropriate ARIA attributes when native semantics are insufficient.

---

# State Management

Prefer:

Local State

↓

Context

↓

Global State

Only elevate state when multiple components require it.

Keep server state separate from UI state.

---

# Performance

Avoid premature optimization.

Use:

* React.memo only when beneficial
* useMemo only when measurable
* useCallback only when necessary

Always:

* Lazy-load heavy components.
* Use stable keys.
* Prevent unnecessary renders.

---

# Code Quality

Generate code that is:

* Readable
* Maintainable
* Self-documenting

Prefer:

* Clear naming
* Small functions
* Pure functions
* Explicit return types

Remove:

* Dead code
* Unused props
* Console logs
* Commented-out code

Comments should explain "why," not "what."

---

# Testing Expectations

Generated components should be easy to test.

Consider:

* User behavior
* Accessibility
* Keyboard navigation
* Edge cases
* Loading states
* Empty states
* Error states

Test behavior rather than implementation details.

---

# Modern UI Principles

Interfaces should be:

* Clean
* Consistent
* Minimal
* Responsive
* Accessible
* Fast

Always provide:

* Loading states
* Empty states
* Error handling
* Success feedback
* Skeleton loaders where appropriate

Avoid layout shifts.

---

# Component Checklist

Before generating a component, ensure:

* Correct Atomic Design layer.
* Single responsibility.
* Reusable and composable.
* TypeScript types defined.
* Accessible (WCAG 2.2 AA).
* Semantic HTML used.
* Keyboard accessible.
* Responsive.
* Proper loading, empty, and error states.
* No unnecessary re-renders.
* Business logic separated from presentation.
* Clean, maintainable code.

---

# Decision Process

When generating code:

1. Reuse existing components before creating new ones.
2. Choose the smallest appropriate Atomic Design layer.
3. Separate business logic into hooks, services, or page components.
4. Prioritize accessibility and semantic HTML.
5. Keep APIs small and intuitive.
6. Optimize only when necessary.
7. Prefer clarity over cleverness.
8. Produce production-ready, enterprise-quality code.
